Package: picam-orchestrator
Architecture: arm64
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, picam-raw, picam-hailo, aipicam-config
Description: MJPEG stream + camera control backend for picam-raw + picam-hailo (Go)
 Headless backend exposing Main / Lores live video as MJPEG
 (multipart/x-mixed-replace over plain HTTP, GET /stream?stream=main|lores),
 plus a JSON status endpoint (/status.json), an on-the-fly OSD toggle
 (/osd), a per-resolution annotation toggle (/annotate), and a
 camera-switch proxy to picam-raw (/camera?id=N). No web interface of
 its own, and never talks to a browser directly — picam-frontend is
 the only client, relaying the stream on to browsers. Each connected
 client selects its stream independently. Main and Lores are live
 feeds straight from picam-raw; either can be switched into a
 delayed+annotated mode that matches detections from picam-hailo by
 timestamp and draws bounding boxes onto the delayed frames. Also
 drives picam-recorder on detection activity and writes JSON event-log
 files alongside recordings. Event snapshot files saved to disk are
 plain JPEG.
 .
 This is a from-scratch Go reimplementation of the original C++
 picam-orchestrator, with the same wire protocols, config file, and
 HTTP/TCP endpoints — see its README for the full behavioral spec. Pure
 Go throughout (no cgo, no C library dependencies) — JPEG encoding uses
 the standard library's own image/jpeg.
 .
 Install picam-frontend on one machine to get a browser UI that can
 view, switch streams/cameras, and toggle the OSD across multiple
 picam-orchestrator backends (e.g. several Pis) from one page.
 .
 Config: /etc/picam-orchestrator/config.ini
